      "writtenOn": "2023-03-13T13:48:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This will make a unique shader for each write swizzle. Is that OK? Do we expect this will bloat the unique shader count?",
      "revId": "a247b7484b304c6bc872e38fbabafaa165cace80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b3033302_be13d741",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-20T16:07:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Based on past conversations with you and Greg, the write swizzle is dependent upon the format of the image, which is pipeline-dependent. So I would at least expect unique pipelines for each write swizzle. \n\nAs far as shaders go, I do think a unique one per each different write swizzle is appropriate (there aren\u0027t many variations). We currently get unique shaders for, say, different output coverages, right? In `SkShaderCodeDictionary::toSkSL(...)`, where we apply the coverage value? I imagine it would be similar to how we handle that. Checking my understanding there.",
      "parentUuid": "51f20c9e_11a2d787",
      "revId": "a247b7484b304c6bc872e38fbabafaa165cace80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b72efd92_c873f521",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-20T17:10:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Fragment coverage is pretty fundamentally complex for and can vary a whole lot for \ndifferent geometry (e.g. see\nhttps://osscs.corp.google.com/skia/skia/+/main:src/gpu/graphite/render/AnalyticRRectRenderStep.cpp;l\u003d781;drc\u003d94d454a12e68f867d0d883900e74f8ffa5a1f6f0 vs https://osscs.corp.google.com/skia/skia/+/main:src/gpu/graphite/render/BitmapTextRenderStep.cpp;l\u003d85;drc\u003d15a8ebfde20a034433101582a919e8c530a7a97b) \n\nI\u0027m not sure it\u0027s really comparable to a write swizzle?\n\nIf we already emit a totally different shader for different image types, then I guess it\u0027s fine (that would make this 1:1 on shader count). I just want to avoid going back to the Ganesh mindset where any minute change to a paint would emit new shaders.",
      "parentUuid": "b3033302_be13d741",
      "revId": "a247b7484b304c6bc872e38fbabafaa165cace80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2fb504a4_f038d345",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-20T18:01:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see, this is a pretty small change in terms of the actual SkSL, but can result in very different colored image outputs (as you can probably imagine). I guess I\u0027m not entirely clear on how one would capture different write swizzles within the scope of one unique shader. I would also like to avoid that Ganesh trap if we can avoid it. :) \n\nIs there an example you could point me to of different behavior being captured within one unique shader? I\u0027m not positive if we *do* already emit a totally different shader for different image types.",
      "parentUuid": "b72efd92_c873f521",
      "revId": "a247b7484b304c6bc872e38fbabafaa165cace80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4f2d8994_1f37f586",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-20T20:01:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I guess I\u0027m not entirely clear on how one would capture different write swizzles within the scope of one unique shader\n\nI\u0027m not entirely sure what you mean, but I would guess it would be something like\n\n```\nuniform int uWriteSwizzle;\n\n//....\n  switch (uWriteSwizzle) {\n    case kRGBA: dest.rgba \u003d color;\n    case kBGRA: dest.bgra \u003d color;\n  }\n  \n```\n\nIf you\u0027re always writing every channel, you can do the write swizzle as a permutation matrix as well: `dest \u003d color * uMatWriteSwizzle;`",
      "parentUuid": "2fb504a4_f038d345",
      "revId": "a247b7484b304c6bc872e38fbabafaa165cace80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "db79568d_1a862be5",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-20T21:46:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well, your concern is potential shader count bloating from each write swizzle, and I am unsure how we would reduce the unique shader count while still supporting all the write swizzles that we need to given that they will be handled by SkSL (as opposed to handled with backend-specific functionalities that use hardware, since not all of our backends can support that. Looking at you, OpenGL ES 2...). Your proposal seems to be storing the write swizzle as a uniform.\n\nFirst, I decided against using a permutation matrix because storing it in that way seemed like overkill for representing just a few possible swizzle options.\n\nNow, as for why I didn\u0027t store the write swizzle as a uniform at all like I had done for read swizzles:\nUniforms in graphite, at least, seem to be organized into render step uniforms (like local matrices, depth which the render step assigns) or shader uniforms (like color for a solid color shader, blend mode for blend shaders,...). Write swizzles are not cleanly associated with a render step (since it\u0027s not dependent upon geometry, but on the destination color type and surface format). \n\nRead swizzles I decided to store as uniforms partially because they are only needed for and associated with a few select shaders that perform sampling. So shaders that need read swizzles can simply have a uniform for that information.\n\nHowever, since a write swizzle is basically the last step to computing the final output color, I (maybe incorrectly!) thought that every shader would need to have access to a write swizzle uniform, which felt like overkill. \n\nWe had talked about jamming the write swizzle into a shader \"footer\" at the end as the final operation or step, but I\u0027m not sure if that would solve the problem of limiting the number of unique shaders generated? LMKWYT.\n\nMaybe we can meet up tomorrow (I\u0027m planning to be in the office) to align on what would be the best path forward. Just wanted to get some thoughts down while they were in my brain. :)",
      "parentUuid": "4f2d8994_1f37f586",
      "revId": "a247b7484b304c6bc872e38fbabafaa165cace80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "96c87ae2_08395b9b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-20T22:28:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m pretty sure I won\u0027t be able to come in tomorrow, but we can GVC chat if you like.\n\nI think I\u0027d want to know Rob/Greg/James\u0027s take on it. My understanding is that one of Graphite\u0027s major tentpole features is fewer shaders; that\u0027s pretty much the biggest feature that Impeller brings to the table, for instance, and it was enough to convince Flutter to develop and fund it. So we need to be very cognizant of the combinatoric burden of every new shader tweak, particularly for simple things like write-swizzles where the cost of being generic in the shader is not high. I would have expected the burden of the uniform to be near zero in these cases; GPUs are AIUI very good at optimizing for uniform control flow, and it\u0027s 4 bytes of uniform data, not enough to matter.\n\nOn the other hand, if we expect that there\u0027s no overlap here--if we know somehow that due to the write swizzle, this shader won\u0027t ever be able to match an existing one regardless--then it\u0027d be fine to bake in the swizzle directly, and it would generate simpler output.",
      "parentUuid": "db79568d_1a862be5",
      "revId": "a247b7484b304c6bc872e38fbabafaa165cace80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "103d26e9_d69584b0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 27
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 18914
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-21T17:25:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Okay, I have spoken with Rob \u0026 Greg and will summarize that discussion output here. I\u0027m happy to set up a brief call with all 4 of us if this requires more discussion after this. As always, thank you for your help, expertise, and patience. :)\n\nRob was supportive of the idea of having the write swizzle be a uniform to cut down on the number of unique shaders *if* that was a plausible option - as in, if storing a write uniform for shaders *could* cut down on the number of unique shaders that would need to be generated.\n\nHowever, Greg was better able to nail down why the uniform approach wouldn’t be helpful in the way we want it to. He assured me that unique shaders created for different write swizzles is to be expected, as we do in Ganesh, and can’t really be avoided. Pipeline creation, not shader creation, is the more expensive operation. The different color attachment formats between pipelines already necessitates different shaders, so we aren’t really altering the number of unique shaders generated one way or another in order to perform this correctly. He even started to question whether read swizzles should be handled in the same way (unique shaders created for each different read swizzle, as opposed to having an enum lookup every time something is sampled with a read swizzle).\n\nLet me know if that makes sense. With that context, I’m inclined to say this approach is what we’d expect.",
      "parentUuid": "96c87ae2_08395b9b",
      "revId": "a247b7484b304c6bc872e38fbabafaa165cace80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
